<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=escalator">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Building and engineering</p><h1 class="enc-title">Escalator</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1896">1896</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as moving staircase</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">An escalator is a moving staircase, a motor-driven chain of linked steps that carries people between floors of a building or structure. Nathan Ames patented &quot;revolving stairs&quot; in 1859, but no working model was built. Jesse W. Reno patented an &quot;Endless Conveyor or Elevator&quot; in 1892 and produced the first working escalator, an inclined belt with cast-iron slats, at the Old Iron Pier on Coney Island, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> City, in 1896. Charles Seeberger teamed with the Otis Elevator Company in 1899 to produce the first commercial escalator, which won first prize at the 1900 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> Exposition Universelle.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/escalator.jpg" alt="Escalator" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chepry 💬 (Andrzej Barabasz) 📷 🇵🇱 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Metro_Centrum_Nauki_Kopernik_Schody-AB.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1896</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Coney Island, New York City</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Jesse W. Reno · Charles Seeberger</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Nathan Ames is credited with patenting the first &quot;escalator&quot; in 1859, but no working model of his design was ever built.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/elevator">Safety elevator<span>1852</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=escalator">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
